1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,342
In May 2026, the CEO of Anthropic said something
almost no CEO ever gets to say.

2
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:15,945
“We tried to plan very well for a world of 10x
growth per year. And yet we saw 80x.”

3
00:00:17,545 --> 00:00:20,690
Five months later, the same company revealed
this number.

4
00:00:21,990 --> 00:00:25,594
A loss of $80.7 billion. In six months.

5
00:00:26,794 --> 00:00:31,971
But before we get to that number, you need to
understand just how strong this company really
is.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,479
Exhibit one: speed.

7
00:00:40,279 --> 00:00:46,365
At the start of 2025, Anthropic’s revenue was
running at about $1 billion a year.

8
00:00:46,865 --> 00:00:48,980
By the end of the year: $9 billion.

9
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:53,315
February: $14 billion. April: $30 billion. May:
$47 billion.

10
00:00:53,815 --> 00:00:58,200
And by the end of July, a run rate of $65
billion a year.

11
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:06,780
Second-quarter revenue alone topped $11.5
billion — nearly 15 times the same quarter a
year earlier.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,217
Exhibit two: it overtook the leader.

13
00:01:10,817 --> 00:01:15,230
For years, OpenAI was the undisputed king of AI
revenue.

14
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:22,322
By August, Anthropic’s run rate was reported at
$65 billion. OpenAI’s: around $40 billion.

15
00:01:22,922 --> 00:01:33,142
In May, investors valued Anthropic at $965
billion — above OpenAI’s last price tag of $852
billion.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,392
Exhibit three: business runs on it.

17
00:01:38,992 --> 00:01:48,921
In a survey by Menlo Ventures — itself an
Anthropic investor — Anthropic took 40% of
enterprise spending on AI models.

18
00:01:48,921 --> 00:01:51,024
OpenAI: 27%. Google: 21%.

19
00:01:51,524 --> 00:01:53,972
In coding, its share was 54%.

20
00:01:54,572 --> 00:02:01,824
Customers paying more than $1 million a year
doubled — from 500 to over 1,000 — in less than
two months.

21
00:02:02,324 --> 00:02:08,020
And its coding agent, Claude Code, became a
multi-billion-dollar business almost overnight.

22
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,712
Exhibit four: the giants pay to be in the room.

23
00:02:13,312 --> 00:02:17,747
Amazon. Google. Microsoft. Nvidia. All four are
investors.

24
00:02:18,247 --> 00:02:25,473
Amazon put in about $13 billion. By June, it
valued that stake at roughly $190 billion.

25
00:02:25,973 --> 00:02:28,109
And the compute is staggering:

26
00:02:28,109 --> 00:02:34,161
up to a million Google TPUs, Amazon’s Project
Rainier, a gigawatt on Microsoft Azure.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,313
Exhibit five: it’s starting to make money.

28
00:02:38,913 --> 00:02:45,751
In both of the first two quarters of 2026,
Anthropic reported positive adjusted operating
income.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,126
Put it all together, and you get one of the
fastest climbs in corporate history.

30
00:02:52,626 --> 00:02:55,870
March 2025: $61.5 billion.

31
00:02:56,220 --> 00:02:57,828
September: $183 billion.

32
00:02:58,178 --> 00:02:59,725
February 2026: $380 billion.

33
00:03:00,075 --> 00:03:02,571
May: $965 billion.

34
00:03:03,071 --> 00:03:06,481
And now, talk of an IPO at up to $2 trillion.

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,443
Then came the draft IPO filing — and three
numbers that made Wall Street do a double take.

36
00:03:18,243 --> 00:03:23,313
Revenue, first half of 2026: $16.3 billion.

37
00:03:24,113 --> 00:03:26,830
Net loss: $80.7 billion.

38
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:33,491
Cash and short-term investments at the end of
2025: $20.28 billion.

39
00:03:34,491 --> 00:03:35,857
Now do the scary math.

40
00:03:36,357 --> 00:03:41,434
$80.7 billion in six months is about $13.5
billion a month.

41
00:03:41,934 --> 00:03:45,789
Twenty billion in the bank runs out in about 45
days.

42
00:03:46,389 --> 00:03:51,495
By that logic, the strongest company in AI
should have been dead by Valentine’s Day.

43
00:03:52,695 --> 00:03:55,060
So which is it? A rocket… or a bomb?

44
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,642
Let’s take that $80.7 billion apart.

45
00:04:03,242 --> 00:04:09,406
About $72 billion of it is a single accounting
line. And not one dollar of it left the company.

46
00:04:10,206 --> 00:04:15,582
It comes from convertible notes: money investors
put in that can later turn into shares.

47
00:04:16,182 --> 00:04:18,138
Imagine a friend lends you $10.

48
00:04:18,138 --> 00:04:23,691
Instead of paying it back in cash, you promise
shares of your company, at today’s price.

49
00:04:24,191 --> 00:04:26,875
Then your company becomes ten times more
valuable.

50
00:04:27,275 --> 00:04:32,094
Those shares are now worth $100. On paper, you
owe your friend $100.

51
00:04:32,494 --> 00:04:37,208
Accounting rules say that $90 jump is a loss.
You didn’t spend a cent.

52
00:04:38,008 --> 00:04:39,414
Now scale it up.

53
00:04:39,414 --> 00:04:46,707
Anthropic’s valuation went from $380 billion to
$965 billion in about three months.

54
00:04:47,207 --> 00:04:52,076
Every leap in value made those notes more
expensive — and the reported loss bigger.

55
00:04:52,576 --> 00:04:57,034
In other words: the stronger Anthropic gets, the
more money it appears to lose.

56
00:04:58,034 --> 00:05:01,288
You can see the other side of this trade on
Amazon’s books.

57
00:05:01,788 --> 00:05:08,435
At the end of March, Amazon valued its Anthropic
convertible notes at $42.2 billion.

58
00:05:08,435 --> 00:05:11,126
By the end of June: $97.9 billion.

59
00:05:11,626 --> 00:05:18,194
A $55.7 billion gain in a single quarter. Same
notes. One company books a gain.

60
00:05:18,194 --> 00:05:19,690
The other, a loss.

61
00:05:20,490 --> 00:05:21,835
And it isn’t new.

62
00:05:21,835 --> 00:05:29,669
Of Anthropic’s roughly $42 billion net loss in
2025, about $34 billion was the same kind of
charge.

63
00:05:30,269 --> 00:05:33,881
Strip it out, and the $80 billion crater shrinks
to under $9 billion.

64
00:05:34,681 --> 00:05:35,909
So the loss is fake?

65
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:39,026
Not so fast.

66
00:05:39,626 --> 00:05:42,993
Because there’s a number in the filing that is
very, very real.

67
00:05:43,793 --> 00:05:44,440
Compute.

68
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:51,560
In the first half of 2025, Anthropic spent $2.3
billion on computing and infrastructure.

69
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,149
A year later: nearly $9 billion.

70
00:05:54,749 --> 00:05:56,834
And that’s just the beginning.

71
00:05:56,834 --> 00:06:04,620
The filing reportedly lists at least $518
billion of AI-infrastructure commitments over
roughly the next decade.

72
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:07,488
Google: $111 billion.

73
00:06:07,788 --> 00:06:10,106
Amazon: $110 billion.

74
00:06:10,406 --> 00:06:12,420
Microsoft: $31 billion.

75
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:17,077
Nvidia GPUs through smaller cloud providers:
about $86 billion.

76
00:06:17,377 --> 00:06:21,651
And equipment leases tied to Broadcom: $161
billion.

77
00:06:22,251 --> 00:06:24,777
Roughly 80% of it can’t be cancelled.

78
00:06:25,577 --> 00:06:27,128
Now put the cash next to it.

79
00:06:28,528 --> 00:06:32,752
$20.28 billion. The bill is about 25 times
bigger.

80
00:06:34,152 --> 00:06:38,005
To be fair, that cash figure is a December
snapshot.

81
00:06:38,005 --> 00:06:44,674
In 2026, Anthropic announced two more rounds:
$30 billion in February, $65 billion in May.

82
00:06:45,174 --> 00:06:47,423
Even so, that’s a fraction of the bill.

83
00:06:48,223 --> 00:06:50,843
And look at who sits on both sides of the table.

84
00:06:51,343 --> 00:06:56,030
Amazon and Google invest in Anthropic. Anthropic
pays them for cloud.

85
00:06:56,030 --> 00:06:59,834
Their stakes rise in value — and Anthropic books
a loss.

86
00:07:00,434 --> 00:07:06,066
The filing even warns that its cloud partners
increasingly see Anthropic as a competitor.

87
00:07:06,666 --> 00:07:11,054
And roughly a fifth to a quarter of its revenue
came from just two customers.

88
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,602
So: is Anthropic strong, or fragile?

89
00:07:17,202 --> 00:07:18,889
It depends on which lens you use.

90
00:07:19,489 --> 00:07:21,354
Lens one: the income statement.

91
00:07:21,354 --> 00:07:26,587
Here, success looks like failure — the higher
the valuation, the bigger the paper loss.

92
00:07:27,187 --> 00:07:30,714
Lens two: operations. Here, the engine is
roaring.

93
00:07:30,714 --> 00:07:35,935
Revenue is compounding, and adjusted operating
income has turned positive.

94
00:07:36,535 --> 00:07:38,021
Lens three: commitments.

95
00:07:38,021 --> 00:07:44,211
Here is the real risk — a half-trillion-dollar
bill that comes due whether customers show up or
not.

96
00:07:45,011 --> 00:07:51,444
The bulls see a company growing faster than
almost anything in history, with demand that
still outruns supply.

97
00:07:51,944 --> 00:07:58,470
The bears see a fixed bill, concentrated
customers, and suppliers who are also rivals.

98
00:07:58,470 --> 00:08:03,630
One research firm says the whole company is
worth just $144 billion.

99
00:08:04,229 --> 00:08:07,312
And that is exactly why Anthropic is going
public.

100
00:08:07,312 --> 00:08:12,059
To keep paying for the future, it needs the
deepest pool of capital on Earth.

101
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,506
So let’s look at those three numbers one more
time.

102
00:08:19,306 --> 00:08:22,548
$16.3 billion in revenue: real demand.

103
00:08:23,248 --> 00:08:27,937
$80.7 billion in losses: mostly a side effect of
success.

104
00:08:28,637 --> 00:08:32,771
$20.28 billion in cash: a snapshot, not a
runway.

105
00:08:33,971 --> 00:08:36,641
Anthropic’s strength is real. So is the bill.

106
00:08:37,541 --> 00:08:40,297
The only question is which one grows faster.
